# Pairs of modules the duplicate-code gate does not report.
#
# **Format.** Two module names per line, in either order, as pylint prints them
# — the file name without `.py` or its directory. Blank lines and lines
# starting with `#` are ignored, so the reason for an entry lives right above
# it. Anything after a `#` on an entry line is also a comment.
#
#     fixtures_a  fixtures_b      # why these two may look alike
#
# **A name here is a basename, and basenames repeat.** A tree can hold two files
# called `conftest.py`. Pylint gives both the same module name, so an entry
# naming one of those could exempt a pair nobody meant. Nothing can fix that from
# here, so an entry naming an ambiguous basename needs its reason to say which
# file it means and why the other one is safe.
#
# **Prefer extracting the shared part.** An entry says two files may resemble
# each other for a reason that will still be true later, and nobody re-reads an
# entry when the reason stops being true.
